# Intent: the firmware plays the right earcon itself for the events only it sees (owner approved 2026-11-13; plan rows 2, 30 to 33, 35). Charger: the 1 s power poll feeds the AXP2101 facts (external power, charging) to the core, which confirms a change over three polls: plug_in, unplug, and charged when charging stops with the cable still in; none at boot, none at night, none when muted or switched off. Link: the reconnect task tells the sound module when the socket drops (down), opens (up) and has been down 10 s (offline, the same moment the Offline words show): the offline earcon plays once per outage, and a return after an outage that sounded plays the success chime. First setup: entering the Wi-Fi setup state plays needs_you at once, at night too (the unit cannot work without a person), and once a minute while it waits; a refused state (below, patch 118) plays it once by day. All of it is queued to the main task (the power poll runs on the esp_timer task, the link on the reconnect task) and goes through the same gate as the host's play_sound. The goodbye cue is a function the power key work calls: the PMIC cuts the power on the hold with no warning the firmware can use, so no call exists yet.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/board/stackchan.cc (MIT); 78/xiaozhi-esp32 main/application.cc and main/protocols/websocket_protocol.cc (MIT). Applies after 116.
# Check after a flash: plug the charger in on a day: two rising notes about 3 s later; unplug: one falling note; leave it until the battery is full: three rising notes once; stop the hub for 15 s: two falling notes at the 10 s mark and the Offline words, then on return the success chime; erase the Wi-Fi: needs_you at once and again each minute; none of these at night (except the setup cue).
